<h2>A harvest, a clash, two FIRs</h2>
<p>The prosecution case: on 12 July 2014, nine accused armed with sticks, a farsa and iron rods attacked the informant while his field was being harvested. Specific overt acts were attributed — petitioner no. 1 allegedly struck the informant&rsquo;s head with a farsa, petitioner no. 3 his hand and chest — and the informant&rsquo;s father and sons were also assaulted. The same episode produced a counter-FIR (Kotwa P.S. Case No. 131 of 2014) from the accused side, the classic signature of a village land-and-crop confrontation with injuries on both sides.</p>

<h2>The long procedural road</h2>
<div class="flow">
  <div class="fstep"><strong>2014:</strong> FIR lodged; the accused say they were given protection under Section 41A CrPC.</div>
  <div class="fstep"><strong>2015:</strong> Chargesheet filed on 18 May 2015.</div>
  <div class="fstep"><strong>2020:</strong> Cognizance taken on 1 February 2020.</div>
  <div class="fstep"><strong>2025-26:</strong> Summons issued 30 July 2025; bailable warrant 9 February 2026; Sessions Judge notes on 29 September 2026 that non-appearance is hampering the trial.</div>
</div>
<p>By the time the matter reached the High Court, two of the nine accused had died, and the doctor&rsquo;s evidence recorded the injuries as simple. The Additional Public Prosecutor opposed the application primarily on the ground of the petitioners&rsquo; delay.</p>

<h2>The court&rsquo;s solution</h2>
<p>The court granted anticipatory bail (para 6), noting the Sessions Judge&rsquo;s own findings on the simple nature of the injuries and that &ldquo;on account of their non-appearance the trial is being hampered.&rdquo; But the grant came with teeth: bonds of Rs 10,000 each with two sureties before the CJM, East Champaran at Motihari; conditions under Section 482(2) BNSS; verification of antecedents by the trial court with cancellation as the sanction for concealment; and a direction to appear for framing of charge within eight weeks (para 7).</p>
<div class="note">
<p><strong>The design of the order:</strong> rather than leaving the trial to limp along on warrants against absentee accused, the High Court used the bail application itself as the lever to secure attendance. The petitioners get protection; the trial gets its accused; and any default collapses the arrangement.</p>
</div>

<h2>What practitioners should note</h2>
<div class="check">
<ul>
  <li>Old cases are not dead cases. Cognizance taken years after the chargesheet still ripens into summons and warrants; ignoring process converts a defensible case into a fugitive problem.</li>
  <li>Medical records drive outcomes in group-clash cases: simple injuries recorded by the doctor soften charges under Sections 307/308 IPC at the bail stage, even where the FIR language is severe.</li>
  <li>Counter-FIRs matter. Cross-cases from the same occurrence signal a two-sided altercation and weigh in favour of protection rather than custody.</li>
  <li>Antecedent-verification conditions are now routine in Patna: concealment of criminal history is an independent ground of cancellation, so disclosure in the petition must be complete.</li>
</ul>
</div>
<p>The order is a reminder that anticipatory bail under the BNSS is not only a shield for the accused; in the hands of a pragmatic court, it is also a scheduling instrument that trades liberty for attendance — and expects the bargain to be kept.</p>
